# What does a 'QALY gained' actually count?
subtitle: "How a QALY weighs each year of life by its quality, and why easing suffering can count as much as adding years."

## What does a 'QALY gained' actually count?
use: qaly_profiles
with:
    box:
      x: 20
      'y': 130
      w: 960
      h: 560
    x_label: Years since diagnosis
    x_max: 14
    profiles:
      - {id: without, label: '', waypoints: [{t: 0, u: 0.6}, {t: 6, u: 0.4}], death_at: 8}
      - {id: with, label: With treatment, waypoints: [{t: 0, u: 0.3}, {t: 1.5, u: 0.7}, {t: 8, u: 0.5}], death_at: 12}
    shade_between:
      a: with
      b: without
    lines: straight
sources:
    - id: zeckhauser1976
      title: Where now for saving lives?
      authors: Zeckhauser R, Shepard D
      year: 1976
      finding: Early peer-reviewed use of the term quality-adjusted life years (Law and Contemporary Problems 40(4):5–45).

    math extra tex "4 \\text{ years} \\times 0.5 = 2 \\text{ QALYs}" x 700 y 560 hidden true
    text yr0 "0" #5a534c x 163 y 165 font_size 22 hidden true
    text yr4 "4" #5a534c x 354 y 165 font_size 22 hidden true
    text yr8 "8" #5a534c x 545 y 165 font_size 22 hidden true
    text u05 "0.5" #5a534c x 135 y 409 font_size 22 hidden true
    text L_wo "Without treatment" #2f6b8f x 330 y 300 font_size 24 hidden true
    text g1 "≈ 1" #3f7d3a x 310 y 442 font_size 32 hidden true
    text g2 "2" #3f7d3a x 640 y 300 font_size 34 hidden true

    card title "What does a 'QALY gained' actually count?"

When a treatment is said to gain QALYs, what exactly is being counted? Let's draw it.
    draw axes

Time runs across. Quality of life runs up, from zero, dead, to one, full health.
    draw full_health_line tick_one tick_zero u05 yr0 yr4 yr8

A patient has just been diagnosed. Without treatment, quality of life starts at 0.6, declines, and the patient dies after eight years.
    draw curve_without L_wo

Each year counts by its quality: a year at 0.6 is 0.6 of a healthy year. The area under the curve, 3.8 here, is the QALYs left.
    highlight curve_without

    pause 0.3

With treatment, the first months are worse: side effects push quality down to 0.3. Then it rises to 0.7, and the patient lives twelve years.
    draw curve_with label_with

That dip is a real loss, shaded red: for a few months the treated patient is worse off.
    draw loss_regions label_loss

But the green is a gain, and it comes in two kinds.
    draw gain_regions label_gain

First, better years: for eight years, net of the red dip, almost one QALY.
    draw g1

Then, extra years. But they are lived at quality 0.5, so four extra years count as two QALYs, not four.
    draw extra g2

Together, about three QALYs gained: 6.65 with treatment against 3.8 without.
    highlight gain_regions color #3f7d3a

So a QALY gained is never just years added: easing pain for years can gain as much as extending life.
    highlight extra part "0.5"

The name dates from a 1976 paper by the economists Richard Zeckhauser and Donald Shepard.
    text zs "Zeckhauser & Shepard, 1976" #5a534c cites ["zeckhauser1976"] x 370 y 598 font_size 26

    quiz "A drug adds four years of life, all lived at quality 0.25. How many QALYs does it add?"
        right: One: four years times 0.25. A QALY weighs every year by how good it is.
        * Four
        + One
        * A quarter
---
